WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:04.078
A good conversation can reveal a barrier. The next

2
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.900
step is to ask whether the process itself makes

3
00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:10.878
that barrier more likely. In our continuing example, a

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.367
team is revising how people hear about a planning

5
00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:16.944
opportunity and how they can respond. The purpose and

6
00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:19.989
deadline are known, while the choice of communication and

7
00:00:20.011 --> 00:00:22.189
participation methods is still open.

8
00:00:22.816 --> 00:00:26.704
Imagine tracing the experience from beginning to end. A

9
00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:30.127
person receives or discovers an invitation, works out what

10
00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:33.371
it means, considers whether they can participate, sends a

11
00:00:33.393 --> 00:00:36.704
response, and learns what happened next. Each step is

12
00:00:36.727 --> 00:00:39.949
connected. If the invitation is hard to understand, a

13
00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:42.771
convenient meeting time alone may not make the opportunity

14
00:00:42.793 --> 00:00:43.349
usable.

15
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.661
If someone can attend but cannot influence the question,

16
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:50.617
attendance alone is not meaningful participation.

17
00:00:51.494 --> 00:00:54.317
You do not need to know everything about a

18
00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:58.272
community before you begin. Start with the process you

19
00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:01.983
can see. Describe what it asks people to do,

20
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.850
what is known about how it works, and whose

21
00:01:04.872 --> 00:01:07.317
experience has not yet been heard.

22
00:01:08.199 --> 00:01:11.354
A community brief may help you prepare a better

23
00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:15.243
question; it cannot tell you whether a particular person

24
00:01:15.266 --> 00:01:18.421
received a notice or why they did not respond.

25
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:23.277
Suppose the current proposal offers one quick live meeting

26
00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:26.299
as the only way to give feedback. List what

27
00:01:26.321 --> 00:01:29.477
a participant would need to do: receive the invitation,

28
00:01:29.832 --> 00:01:33.432
understand it, find time, use the meeting format, and

29
00:01:33.454 --> 00:01:36.499
contribute while the meeting is taking place. Then consider

30
00:01:36.521 --> 00:01:39.677
what remains unknown. Do people need another language or

31
00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:40.143
format?

32
00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:45.512
Could work schedules, caregiving, transportation, technology, or the time

33
00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:49.223
needed to think affect participation? Which conditions are supported

34
00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:51.823
by evidence, and which are questions to ask?

35
00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.501
Avoid assuming that any barrier applies to a person

36
00:01:56.523 --> 00:02:00.123
because of a community label. A live meeting may

37
00:02:00.146 --> 00:02:02.701
work well for some people and poorly for others

38
00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:06.390
within the same community. Ask people affected what would

39
00:02:06.412 --> 00:02:09.279
help, and make sure input reaches beyond the people

40
00:02:09.301 --> 00:02:12.679
the team already knows. Pay attention to who has

41
00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:16.212
the authority, time, and resources to change the process.

42
00:02:16.956 --> 00:02:20.622
Also examine the team's side of the process. What

43
00:02:20.644 --> 00:02:23.466
will happen to responses, who will review them, and

44
00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:26.333
what can still change? Adding a new way to

45
00:02:26.356 --> 00:02:29.533
respond is useful only if it is usable and

46
00:02:29.556 --> 00:02:32.356
the team can consider the input within the actual

47
00:02:32.378 --> 00:02:37.489
decision process. Name limits honestly instead of promising a

48
00:02:37.511 --> 00:02:38.844
choice that is not available.

49
00:02:40.032 --> 00:02:43.321
A second response route might include an accessible written

50
00:02:43.343 --> 00:02:47.632
option, a telephone option, a supported conversation, or another

51
00:02:47.654 --> 00:02:51.832
method people identify as useful. These are possibilities, not

52
00:02:51.854 --> 00:02:54.410
automatic requirements or a promise that all can be

53
00:02:54.432 --> 00:02:59.966
offered immediately. Check accessibility, language assistance, privacy, the deadline

54
00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:02.743
and the program's current procedures before deciding what to

55
00:03:02.766 --> 00:03:03.232
recommend.

56
00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:07.421
Compare the alternatives against the purpose of the opportunity.

57
00:03:08.243 --> 00:03:11.599
Who can use each option? What extra effort does

58
00:03:11.621 --> 00:03:14.954
it place on participants and staff? What evidence do

59
00:03:14.977 --> 00:03:17.154
you have, and what would you need to learn?

60
00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:20.710
If a change requires approval, bring a clear proposal

61
00:03:20.732 --> 00:03:22.266
to the responsible person.

62
00:03:22.951 --> 00:03:26.084
Do not treat a community description as a substitute

63
00:03:26.107 --> 00:03:29.929
for a required equity analysis or as authority to

64
00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:30.840
change a policy.

65
00:03:31.898 --> 00:03:35.387
The Equity Analysis Toolkit can help organize the purpose,

66
00:03:35.676 --> 00:03:39.831
people affected, evidence, alternatives and review. Use the quick

67
